        "elapsedMs": 14309,
        "text": "Since you’ve been using **Adobe Premiere Pro**, I’d start with resources that teach both the software and the craft of editing:\n\n### Free tutorials\n- **[Adobe Learn](https://www.adobe.com/learn/premiere-pro)** — Official lessons on trimming, audio, captions, color correction, and exporting. A good foundation, especially when you want guidance that matches the software.\n- **[Premiere Gal](https://www.youtube.com/@PremiereGal)** — Practical Premiere tutorials covering everyday workflows, effects, audio cleanup, and captions.\n- **[Justin Odisho](https://www.youtube.com/@JustinOdisho)** — Focused demonstrations of Premiere techniques and effects. Useful when you want to learn one specific skill at a time.\n- **[This Guy Edits](https://www.youtube.com/@ThisGuyEdits)** — More about *why* an edit works: pacing, emotion, choosing performances, and storytelling rather than just buttons and menus.\n\n### Structured courses\n- **LinkedIn Learning** — Look for a recent *Premiere Pro Essential Training* course. It’s paid, but some public libraries provide free access.\n- **[Blackmagic Design training](https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/products/davinciresolve/training)** — Free DaVinci Resolve lessons and project files. Worth considering if you want to explore another editor, though there’s no need to switch from Premiere to learn well.\n\n### A book on the craft\n- **_In the Blink of an Eye_ by Walter Murch** — A short, thoughtful introduction to choosing cuts based on emotion, story, and rhythm. It’s not a software manual.\n\n**A manageable place to start:** Make a 30–60-second video from your own footage. First focus on clean cuts and pacing, then clear audio, then basic correction in Premiere’s **Lumetri Color** panel. Following a tutorial with your own footage usually teaches more than watching several in a row.\n\nAre you most interested in storytelling, color grading, or editing faster?"
      },
